    "grade": "7-8",
    "points": 5,
    "timeMinutes": null,
    "type": "mc",
    "prompt": "In the trapezoid $ABCD$, the points $M$ and $N$ are the midpoints of the sides $BC$ and $CD$, respectively. The area of $ABCD$ is $32\\text{ cm}^2$ and the length of $AD$ is $\\frac13$ of the length of $BC$. What is the area of the triangle $AMN$, in $\\text{cm}^2$?",
    "image": "qimages/kangaroo/ca-2015-g0708/q24.webp",
    "choices": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "text": "8"
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "text": "10"
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "text": "12"
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "text": "15"
      },
      {
        "key": "E",
        "text": "16"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "B",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Subtract the outside triangles",
        "body": "Let $AD = a$, $BC = 3a$ and the height be $h$. Area of $ABCD = \\frac{a + 3a}{2} h = 2ah = 32$, so $ah = 16$.\n\nTriangle $AMN$ is the trapezoid minus three triangles:\n- $ABM$: base $BM = \\frac{3a}{2}$, height $h$: area $\\frac34 ah = 12$.\n- $MCN$: $N$ is the midpoint of $CD$, so it lies halfway between the parallel lines $AD$ and $BC$: its height above $BC$ is $\\frac h2$; base $MC = \\frac{3a}{2}$: area $\\frac12 \\cdot \\frac{3a}{2}\\cdot\\frac h2 = \\frac38 ah = 6$.\n- $ADN$: base $AD = a$, and $N$ is $\\frac h2$ below $AD$: area $\\frac12 \\cdot a \\cdot \\frac h2 = \\frac14 ah = 4$.\n\nArea $AMN = 32 - 12 - 6 - 4 = 10$.\n\n**Answer: (B) 10**"
      },
      {
        "title": "Coordinates",
        "body": "Take $BC = 3$, $AD = 1$; then $\\frac{1+3}{2}h = 32$ gives $h = 16$. Put $B=(0,0)$, $C=(3,0)$, $A=(p,16)$, $D=(p+1,16)$. Then $M = (1.5, 0)$ and $N = (\\frac{p+4}{2}, 8)$.\n\n$\\vec{AM} = (1.5 - p, -16)$, $\\vec{AN} = (\\frac{4-p}{2}, -8)$. Area $= \\frac12|(1.5-p)(-8) - (-16)\\frac{4-p}{2}| = \\frac12|-12 + 8p + 32 - 8p| = 10$, for every $p$.\n\n**Answer: (B) 10**"
      }
    ],
