    "type": "mc",
    "prompt": "Three cats, Tom, Bob and Rob, kept a record on the number of mice each of them caught for three consecutive days. Each day, Tom caught twice as many mice as in the previous day; Bob caught two more mice than in the previous day, while Rob caught the same number of mice each day. In the end, it appeared that each cat caught the same number of mice in total. At least how many mice did Tom, Rob and Bob catch together in the first day?",
    "image": null,
    "choices": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "text": "9"
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "text": "15"
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "text": "10"
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "text": "18"
      },
      {
        "key": "E",
        "text": "12"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "B",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Write the totals",
        "body": "If on day 1 Tom caught $t$, Bob $b$ and Rob $r$ mice, the totals are\n- Tom: $t + 2t + 4t = 7t$,\n- Bob: $b + (b+2) + (b+4) = 3b + 6$,\n- Rob: $3r$.\n\nAll are equal, so $7t$ is a multiple of 3, hence $t$ is a multiple of 3. $t = 0$ would give $3b + 6 = 0$, impossible. So the smallest is $t = 3$: total 21, $b = 5$, $r = 7$.\n\nOn the first day they caught $3 + 5 + 7 = 15$ mice. Larger $t$ ($6, 9, \\dots$) gives more mice on every day.\n\n**Answer: (B) 15**"
      }
    ],
